### What is keyed state?

Keyed state is only available on a keyed stream, after a `keyBy`. Flink automatically scopes the state to the current key, so your code reads and writes "the state" and Flink routes it to the right key's slice.

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.*;
import org.apache.flink.configuration.Configuration;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.KeyedProcessFunction;
import org.apache.flink.util.Collector;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BalanceTracker
        extends KeyedProcessFunction<String, Transaction, String> {

    private transient ValueState<BigDecimal> balanceState;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration params) {
        ValueStateDescriptor<BigDecimal> desc =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("balance", BigDecimal.class);
        balanceState = getRuntimeContext().getState(desc);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Transaction txn, Context ctx,
            Collector<String> out) throws Exception {
        BigDecimal current = balanceState.value();
        if (current == null) current = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal updated = current.add(txn.getAmount());
        balanceState.update(updated);
        out.collect(txn.getAccountId() + ": " + updated);
    }
}
```

```text
Input:  ACC-001 +50000, ACC-002 +30000, ACC-001 +20000
Output: ACC-001: 50000
        ACC-002: 30000
        ACC-001: 70000  (running balance updated)
```

### What are the keyed state primitives?

Flink provides five keyed state types, each suited to different patterns.

| State Type | Stores | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| `ValueState<T>` | Single value per key | Running balance, last seen timestamp |
| `ListState<T>` | List per key | Buffered events, transaction history |
| `MapState<K,V>` | Map per key | Per-account sub-keyed lookups |
| `ReducingState<T>` | Single value, incrementally combined | Running sum |
| `AggregatingState<IN,OUT>` | Like Reducing but input/output differ | Custom aggregation |

```java
// ListState: buffer recent transactions per account
ListStateDescriptor<Transaction> listDesc =
    new ListStateDescriptor<>("recentTxns", Transaction.class);
ListState<Transaction> recentTxns =
    getRuntimeContext().getListState(listDesc);
recentTxns.add(txn);  // append to list for current key

// MapState: per-account merchant spend tracking
MapStateDescriptor<String, BigDecimal> mapDesc =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("merchantSpend",
        String.class, BigDecimal.class);
MapState<String, BigDecimal> merchantSpend =
    getRuntimeContext().getMapState(mapDesc);
merchantSpend.put(txn.getMerchantId(), newTotal);
```

### What is operator state?

Operator state is scoped to one parallel subtask and does not depend on a key. It is used by sources and sinks for per-worker plumbing like offset tracking and buffer management.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.checkpoint.CheckpointedFunction;
import org.apache.flink.runtime.state.FunctionInitializationContext;
import org.apache.flink.runtime.state.FunctionSnapshotContext;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ListState;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ListStateDescriptor;

public class OffsetTrackingSource
        implements CheckpointedFunction {

    private transient ListState<Long> offsetState;
    private long currentOffset = 0;

    @Override
    public void initializeState(FunctionInitializationContext ctx)
            throws Exception {
        ListStateDescriptor<Long> desc =
            new ListStateDescriptor<>("offsets", Long.class);
        offsetState = ctx.getOperatorStateStore()
            .getListState(desc);
        // Restore offset from checkpoint
        for (Long offset : offsetState.get()) {
            currentOffset = offset;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void snapshotState(FunctionSnapshotContext ctx)
            throws Exception {
        offsetState.clear();
        offsetState.add(currentOffset);
    }
}
```

### How is keyed state different from operator state?

| Aspect | Keyed State | Operator State |
|---|---|---|
| Scope | Per key (after keyBy) | Per parallel subtask |
| Access | Within key context only | Within operator instance |
| Rescaling | Redistributed by key group | ListState: round-robin; Union: full copy |
| Primitives | Value, List, Map, Reducing, Aggregating | ListState, UnionListState, BroadcastState |
| Use case | Per-entity: balances, counters, sessions | Per-worker: offsets, buffers, rules |

```java
// Keyed state: per-account balance (after keyBy)
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new BalanceTracker());  // ValueState per account

// Operator state: source offset tracking (no keyBy)
// Source reads partitions and remembers its own read position
```

### What is BroadcastState?

BroadcastState is a special map-based operator state used with a broadcast stream. A small, slowly-changing dataset like fraud rules is replicated identically to every parallel instance.

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.MapStateDescriptor;

MapStateDescriptor<String, FraudRule> ruleDescriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("rules", String.class, FraudRule.class);

BroadcastStream<FraudRule> broadcastRules =
    ruleStream.broadcast(ruleDescriptor);

// Every parallel instance gets an identical copy of the rules
DataStream<String> alerts = txnStream
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .connect(broadcastRules)
    .process(new FraudScreeningFunction());
```

### How does rescaling work?

Keyed state rescales gracefully by redistributing key groups across the new parallelism. Operator state requires explicit handling: ListState redistributes round-robin, UnionListState gives every instance the full set.

```java
// Keyed state: parallelism 4 → 8
// Key groups automatically redistributed
// ACC-001 moves from task 2 to task 5 with its state intact

// Operator ListState: parallelism 2 → 4
// Elements split round-robin: [A,B,C,D] → task0:[A,C] task1:[B,D]

// Operator UnionListState: parallelism 2 → 4
// Every task gets full set: [A,B,C,D] → all tasks get [A,B,C,D]
```

### When do you use each?

Use keyed state for anything naturally per-entity: per-account balances, per-card fraud counters, per-customer sessions. Use operator state for per-worker plumbing: source read positions, sink buffers, and broadcast rules.

```java
// Keyed state: per-entity logic
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new BalanceTracker());     // ValueState per account

stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getCardId())
    .process(new FraudCounter());       // MapState per card

// Operator state: per-worker plumbing
// Source: remembers offsets (ListState)
// Sink: buffers rows for batch commit (ListState)
// Rules: broadcast to all instances (BroadcastState)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using operator state for per-entity logic, which does not partition by key and breaks when parallelism changes. Another mistake is forgetting that BroadcastState must be small because it is fully replicated.

```java
// WRONG -- using operator state for per-account balance
// Not partitioned by key! All accounts on same subtask.
// Rescaling loses data.

// CORRECT -- use keyed state after keyBy
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new BalanceTracker());  // ValueState per account
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keyed state is far more common and rescales gracefully by key group. Operator state requires you to think about how contents redistribute on parallelism changes. BroadcastState must be kept small because it is fully replicated. Both kinds are snapshotted into checkpoints for exactly-once recovery, and the choice of state backend determines whether state lives on the JVM heap or in an embedded on-disk store.
